  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (Through Thursday)...\n\nWhile mostly dry conditions will prevail through this evening, \nsome weak energy moving in from the southwest may provide enough \nforcing to generate high-based showers and a weak thundershower or\ntwo late today (15 percent chance). With this, gusty and erratic \nwinds may develop with dry air in place near the surface.\n\nOtherwise...the main weather story is heat. Models agree on a \npowerful ridge dominating our region this weekend with 700mb \ntemperatures near or exceeding 20C by Sunday, and 850 mb \ntemperatures of 35-40C. High temperatures of 95 to 112 degrees are\nforecast, warmest on Sunday. The hottest locations will likely be\nfrom about Billings eastward where there is a moderate chance of \nexceeding 110 degrees. All-time record highs are possible Sunday.\nAlong with the hot daytime temperatures, overnight low \ntemperatures in the 60s and 70s, warmest Sunday night, will \nexacerbate the risk of heat stress.\n\nAlong with the heat, very dry conditions will exist, with \nrelative humidity values decreasing into the single digits to \nteens Saturday and Sunday, and teens to 20s percent Monday. \nBreezy south to southwest winds look to develop over western and \ncentral areas (gusts 20s to 30s mph) during the day on Sunday, \nahead of a cold front from the northwest Sunday evening/night. The\ncombination of breezy winds, record temperatures, and extremely \nlow humidities have led us to issue a Fire Weather Watch for\nSunday across the nearly all the lower elevations of our CWA. \nPlease take extra care not to cause a spark.\n\nEnsembles agree on shifting the ridge east a bit by midweek \nallowing temperatures to cool slightly (highs in the upper 80s and\n90s), and monsoonal moisture to move in from the southwest. This \nwould result in a daily chance of showers/thunderstorms to return \nto the area along with higher relative humidity values. Ensembles\nsuggest remarkable PWATs reaching up to 1.50 inches for much of \nthe area from Tuesday onward. Any decent impulse in the monsoonal \nflow may result in storms with very heavy rainfall. We will need \nto monitor those PWAT trends closely heading into next week. \n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F\n\nBT",
